Usage: dir [OPTION]... [FILE]...

List information about files (the current directory by default).
dir is equivalent to ls -C; it lists files in columns.

Mandatory arguments to long options are mandatory for short options too.

  -a, --all                  do not ignore entries starting with .
  -A, --almost-all           do not list implied . and ..
  -b, --escape               print C-style escapes for nongraphic characters
  -B, --ignore-backups       do not list implied entries ending with ~
  -C                         list entries by columns (default)
  -d, --directory            list directories themselves, not their contents
  -F, --classify             append indicator (one of */=>@|) to entries
  -g                         like -l, but do not list owner
  -h, --human-readable       with -l and -s, print sizes like 1K 234M 2G
  -i, --inode                print the index number of each file
  -l                         use a long listing format
  -m                         fill width with a comma separated list of entries
  -n, --numeric-uid-gid      like -l, but list numeric user and group IDs
  -o                         like -l, but do not list group information
  -p                         append / indicator to directories
  -q, --hide-control-chars   print ? instead of nongraphic characters
  -Q, --quote-name           enclose entry names in double quotes
  -r, --reverse              reverse order while sorting
  -R, --recursive            list subdirectories recursively
  -s, --size                 print the allocated size of each file, in blocks
  -S                         sort by file size, largest first
  -t                         sort by time, newest first
  -u                         with -lt: sort by, and show, access time
  -U                         do not sort; list entries in directory order
  -v                         natural sort of (version) numbers within text
  -w, --width=COLS           assume screen is COLS wide
  -x                         list entries by lines across
  -X                         sort alphabetically by entry extension
  -1                         list one file per line

dir is a wrapper around ls with -C (columns) as the default format.

OPTIONS:
  -a, --all                                       do not ignore entries starting
                                                  with .
  -A, --almost-all                                do not list implied . and ..
  -b, --escape                                    print C-style escapes for
                                                  nongraphic characters
  -B, --ignore-backups                            do not list implied entries
                                                  ending with ~
  -C                                              list entries by columns
  -d, --directory                                 list directories themselves,
                                                  not their contents
  -F, --classify                                  append indicator (one of
                                                  */=>@|) to entries
  -g                                              like -l, but do not list owner
  -h, --human-readable                            with -l and -s, print sizes
                                                  like 1K 234M 2G etc.
  -i, --inode                                     print the index number of each
                                                  file
  -l                                              use a long listing format
      --long                                      use a long listing format
      --long-list                                 use a long listing format
  -m                                              fill width with a comma
                                                  separated list of entries
  -n, --numeric-uid-gid                           like -l, but list numeric user
                                                  and group IDs
  -o                                              like -l, but do not list group
                                                  information
  -p                                              append / indicator to
                                                  directories
  -q, --hide-control-chars                        print ? instead of nongraphic
                                                  characters
  -Q, --quote-name                                enclose entry names in double
                                                  quotes
  -r, --reverse                                   reverse order while sorting
  -R, --recursive                                 list subdirectories
                                                  recursively
  -s, --size                                      print the allocated size of
                                                  each file, in blocks
  -S                                              sort by file size, largest
                                                  first
  -t                                              sort by time, newest first
  -T, --tabsize ARG                               assume tab stops at each COLS
                                                  instead of 8
  -u                                              with -lt: sort by, and show,
                                                  access time
  -U                                              do not sort; list entries in
                                                  directory order
  -v                                              natural sort of (version)
                                                  numbers within text
  -w, --width ARG                                 assume screen is instead of
                                                  COLS wide
  -x                                              list entries by lines across
  -X                                              sort alphabetically by entry
                                                  extension
  -1                                              list one file per line
      --sort ARG                                  sort by WORD: none (-U), size
                                                  (-S), time (-t), version (-v),
                                                  extension (-X)
      --format ARG                                across (-x), commas (-m),
                                                  horizontal (-x), long (-l),
                                                  single-column (-1), verbose
                                                  (-l), vertical (-C)
      --time ARG                                  show time as WORD instead of
                                                  default: atime, access, use,
                                                  ctime, status
      --color ARG                                 colorize the output: always,
                                                  auto, never
      --group-directories-first                   group directories before files
      --author                                    show author in long format
      --block-size ARG                            scale sizes by SIZE
  -Z, --context                                   print any security context of
                                                  each file
  -L, --dereference                               when showing file information
                                                  for a symbolic link, show
                                                  information for the file the
                                                  link references
  -H, --dereference-command-line                  follow symlinks listed on the
                                                  command line
      --dereference-command-line-symlink-to-dir   follow each command-line
                                                  symlink to a directory
      --dereference-command-line-symlinks-to-dir  follow each command-line
                                                  symlink to a directory
  -D, --dired                                     generate output designed for
                                                  Emacs dired mode
      --file-type                                 append file type indicators,
                                                  without *
      --hide ARG                                  do not list implied entries
                                                  matching PATTERN
      --hyperlink[=ARG]                           hyperlink file names when
                                                  outputting to a terminal
  -I, --ignore ARG                                do not list implied entries
                                                  matching PATTERN
      --indicator-style ARG                       append indicator using WORD
  -N, --literal                                   print entry names without
                                                  quoting
  -G, --no-group                                  in a long listing, don't print
                                                  group names
      --quoting-style ARG                         use quoting style WORD
      --show-control-chars                        show nongraphic characters
                                                  as-is in file names
      --si                                        like -h, but use powers of
                                                  1000 not 1024
      --time-style ARG                            time/date format with -l (e.g.
                                                  full-iso, long-iso, iso,
                                                  locale, +FORMAT)
      --full-time                                 like -l --time-style=full-iso
  -c                                              with -lt: sort by, and show,
                                                  ctime; otherwise: sort by
                                                  ctime, newest first
  -f                                              list all entries in directory
                                                  order
  -k, --kibibytes                                 default to 1024-byte blocks
                                                  for file system usage
      --zero                                      end each output line with NUL,
                                                  not newline
      --help                                      display this help and exit
  -V, --version                                   output version information and
                                                  exit

EXIT STATUS:
  0  if OK,
  1  if minor problems,
  2  if serious trouble.

WinuxCmd is a Windows implementation of GNU CoreUtils for Linux-Windows developers and AI coding assistants.
